---
title: Checks
summary: Structural checks that fail the build instead of the visitor. The link audit resolves every in-prose link against the site's address space, and reports a broken one as a named error at prerender and when you open the page in dev.
related:
  - content/site
  - content/outline
  - reference/constraints
---

A broken internal link is a defect you would rather find yourself at build time than have a visitor find at runtime. Site checks run over the whole corpus as **data**, report what they find as named findings, and can fail the build. The link audit is built in, and you can add checks of your own.

Any site with cross-references, which means any docs site, should turn on `links()`. It is what lets you rename and reorganize pages without worrying, because when you move a page, every stale link to it fails the build with a clear error.

## The link audit

Pass `checks` to `site()`, and `links()` resolves every markdown link in every body against the site's address space:

```ts
export const docs = site({ outline: docs, checks: [links()] });
```

A link to a page that does not exist, or to an `#anchor` that no heading produces, is a finding. This site runs the audit, so every cross-link in this section was verified by it; while one of these pages linked to a sibling that had not been written yet, the build refused to pass until the sibling existed.

It reports in two places:

- **At prerender**, a broken link fails the build with the page, the link text and the target.
- **In dev**, the same finding appears when you open the page, so you catch it while you write.

The audit knows the _whole_ address space, including [dimension](/docs/content/dimensions) coordinates and declared redirects, so it can tell a genuinely dead link from one that resolves through an alias.

## Findings as data

`docs.check()` runs every check over the corpus and returns the findings as plain data without ever throwing, so you can assert on them in a test or a CI step independently of a prerender crawl:

```ts
// a vitest guard: the docs have no broken links
import { docs } from '$lib/site.server';

test('no broken links', async () => {
	const findings = await docs.check({ base: '/docs' });
	expect(findings).toEqual([]);
});
```

This matters most on a dynamic site, where the prerender crawler never runs and this check is your only structural safeguard.

## Custom checks

A check is a named object with a `page` hook, a `site` hook or both, each returning findings. `page` runs per slug inside `load`, so dev reports it when you open the page and an `error`-severity finding throws. `site` runs once over the whole corpus, through `docs.check()`:

```ts title=$lib/checks.ts
// a house rule: every page fills its summary
import type { Check, Finding } from '@ogygia/content';

export function requireSummary(): Check {
	return {
		name: 'require-summary',
		async page(slug, { outline, ctx }): Promise<Finding[]> {
			const resolved = await outline.resolve(slug, ctx);
			if (!resolved) return [];
			const entry = await resolved.collection.get(resolved.record.entryId, ctx);
			if (!entry || typeof entry.data.summary === 'string') return [];
			return [{
				check: 'require-summary',
				severity: 'error',
				slug,
				message: `'${slug}' has no summary — the docs index and llms.txt read it.`
			}];
		}
	};
}
```

```ts
import { links } from '@ogygia/content';
import { requireSummary } from '$lib/checks';

export const docs = site({ outline: docs, checks: [links(), requireSummary()] });
```

Severity decides what happens. `'error'` throws from `load`, which fails the build at prerender and blocks the page in dev, while `'warn'` shows up in the `docs.check()` findings and never blocks a render.

::: details Checks vs. structural build errors
These are two deliberately separate layers. **Structural invariants** (an orphan entry, a slug collision, an inconsistent `NN-` sequence) are [outline](/docs/content/outline) build errors that are always on and cannot be configured away, because a site that has them is not well-formed. **Checks** are _content policy_: pluggable, per site and set by severity. `links()` is policy, so whether a deliberately dead link during a migration is allowed, tolerated as `warn` or forbidden is your decision.
:::

## Do / don't

- **Do** run `links()` from the start. It costs nothing and makes every reorganization safe.
- **Do** assert on `docs.check()` in CI for a dynamic site, where it stands in for the structural check the prerender crawler would otherwise give you.
- **Don't** treat a finding as noise. A broken link or a missing anchor is a real defect that a reader would run into, so fix the link or the target.
- **Don't** wait for production to find a dead cross-reference, when the build and your dev server can tell you first.

